00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.250 align:start position:0%
 
you know there was more evidence today  of the impact that rising prices are  beginning to have thanks to the weakness  of sterling since last June's referendum  vote UK retail sales fell by 0.4% on  unlike for like basis last month  compared with February last year while  over the three months to February  non-food sales also fell by 0.4% and  that's the first quarterly decline for  more than five years  those figures were compiled for the  British Retail Consortium by KPMG whose  head of retail Paul Martin joins us and  Tim Rycroft is Corporate Affairs  Director at the food and drink  Association which today reported rapidly  declining confidence among its members  due to price rises and it's calling for  help from the Chancellor in tomorrow's  budget Paul let me start with you it's  this conclusive proof then that consumer  confidence is weakening good evening in  yes we are seeing the first signs of  consumer spending slowing down as our  reports with the BRC show today are  there any areas of non-food that are  bucking the trend at all I'm in half  term Cola in February for example it is  interesting you say that specifically  the furniture category actually did  pretty well over the trading period we  reported on but that is actually quite  normal a number of other categories had  quite a tough time are we overdoing the  doom and gloom I mean I was looking back  to 2011 when we last saw fall like this  and on that occasion we had falling  crisis we had disinflation now we've got  inflation I mean it's not as bad as it  was in 2011 is it overdoing the doom and  gloom it's tough it is not impossible  out there I think what is really  important to emphasize though we are  seeing a perfect storm out there in the  market we are seeing the devaluation of  sterling we are seeing a business rate  conversation that has been all over the  headlines we are seeing the national  living wage we are seeing the  apprenticeship levy to come into for  which and very soon and that really is  resulting with some significantly  increased pressures to the sector but in  fairness our consumers not behaving nota  CLE here I mean they were all told  towards the end of last year prices were  going to go up in the new year there was  evidence Simon Amin but talking to  people I said James at Dixon's Carphone  he said there was evidence of people  bringing forward purchases from now into  last autumn logically that seems have  been what's happened isn't it  partly some consumers have definitely  reacted that way but the consumer is  there is no one-size-fits-all and some  consumers would have brought purchases  forward but in certain categories you  don't buy things months and months ahead  so we are definitely seeing the  pressures being translated from the high  street into the consumers pockets well  Tim let me bring you in at this point  why you all members so downbeat right  now I think there's two things I think  in the real world that we've just heard  about conditions are very tough  two-thirds of our members are saying  their margins are being squeezed 40%  saying they're feeling more gloomy than  they were in October I think partly  that's also due to the fact that we're  in a continued period of uncertainty and  where we're now waiting for article 50  to be triggered so that we can actually  move into a time when we start to  resolve some of these outstanding issues  around immigration and trade hoping to  see in the budget that tomorrow well I  think there are two really important  things that we'd like to see the first  is help for exports so we had a great  year for exports in 2016 20 billion  pound barrier broken for the first time  for food and drink but still only one in  five food and drink companies export so  we'd love to see support particularly  for small and medium sized businesses to  help them understand how they can crack  export markets that would make a really  big difference we look across the Irish  Sea the Irish government does this very  well for example there's also export  promotion help in Scotland and Wales so  we'd love to see UK food and drink  really get that boost and you know put  it to Tim I mean again are you not  overdoing the doom and gloom slightly if  I look elsewhere new report nearly two  thirds of your members expect as he  sells in the UK grow over coming months  and the same number see sales outside  that e the UK growing this well I mean  that isn't too bad it's absolutely not  I think our industry is incredibly  resourceful and resilient they're  looking for opportunity so where export  where people have imported ingredients  they're now looking for domestic  suppliers that's a whole new opportunity  for domestic food producers if they  haven't been exporting much they're  seeing that as an opportunity they're  always looking for new ways to to find a  way through these difficulties and what  about this young man you touched earlier  on on the price war with the  supermarkets I mean interestingly though  we are still seeing some prefer prices  falling I mean things like come  eggsontour  and bacon understand  it's quite a mixed picture today isn't  it of some commodities that have gone up  and some gone down I think we're still  expecting though over the course of 2017  that food price inflation will become  more general people will have to get  used to that and we've seen today that  there's market share of the discounters  has gone up and I think that's because  customers consumers are now actually  shopping around much more actively than  perhaps they used it I should I should  make them make advantage of carpet cheek  R&amp;R and now while I can that well what  are your members hoping to see from the  retail is hoping to see from the budget  to one you touched on business rate so  there are other things that you're  hoping that the government may weigh  back on I mean touch on the levy there I  think there'll be a number of points  that the retailers will hope that these  will be addressed tomorrow but obviously  the business rate conversation is top of  mind will there be a strategic answer to  that question potentially not there will  likely be some tactical conversations  but the industry is undergoing a  significant structural change and that  really does need to be addressed by the  government going forward we are seeing a  significant increase again as our data  showed today of online penetration more  and more sales are actually being  generated through digital channels and  that is a channel that is not really  being addressed in this conversation at  the moment and so the onward march to  online continues unabated them not  unabated so that's another important  point in the data that we broadcast  today we have seen a slowdown of the  growth in online but it is becoming an  ever-increasing channel for UK retail  whilst at the same time we shouldn't  forget that nearly 80 percent of all  sales are still generated through stores

